  "_purpose": "Phrase-to-prefix matching rules, calibrated against QElectroTech's own qet_labels.xml -- which is the project's hand-curated designation data and therefore the ground truth for what each letter means here. Kept as reviewable data rather than buried in Python. Matched longest-phrase-first (most specific wins) after lowercasing and stripping accents; QET's element names are heavily French, so French terms sit alongside English ones rather than being an afterthought. Run tools/iec81346/score.py after any edit: it checks these rules against every category qet_labels.xml already assigns a prefix to.",
  "_convention_note": "These codes follow the classic DIN 40719 / IEC 60617-style electrical set that qet_labels.xml actually uses, NOT the abstract 'inherent function' table of IEC 81346-2:2019. The two disagree substantially -- 81346-2:2019 would call a sensor B and a contact Q, where this project uses P and S. An earlier version of this tooling was calibrated to the 2019 table and consequently contradicted the curated data on 62% of the categories they both covered. Match the project, not the edition of the standard you happen to be reading.",
  "_suppression_note": "code: null marks a phrase that must NOT resolve to a prefix -- used to stop a shorter, lower-priority rule matching inside it (e.g. 'switch on'/'switch off' block the bare 'switch' rule from firing on 'switch-off delay timer', where 'switch' is part of a verb phrase about timing direction, not a noun naming a switch). match() already treats a null code as 'no rule matched', so the normal fallback tiers still run.",
